# Fibres of multiplication by n are finite

Theorem statement: `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.fibSet_finite` (`d1b840cd-018f-5bca-9508-90b9fb006ef1`),
by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `6812f5c9-90dd-56c9-99cf-10d3c43d2e28`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $F$ and $K$ be fields with $K$ an $F$-algebra and $K$ algebraically closed, let $W$ be a
Weierstrass curve over $F$ which is elliptic, and let $n$ be a natural number whose image in $K$ is
nonzero. Write $(W⁄K)$ for the base change of $W$ to $K$ and $(W⁄K)$`.Point` for its group of affine
points together with the point at infinity. Then for every point $Q$ of $(W⁄K)$, the set `fibSet W K
n Q`, that is $\{P : n \cdot P = Q\}$, the fibre over $Q$ of multiplication by the integer $n$ on
$(W⁄K)$`.Point`, is finite. No claim is made about the cardinality of this fibre, nor about its
non-emptiness; the assertion is finiteness alone, and in particular it holds vacuously when $Q$ is
not divisible by $n$.

This is the finiteness half of the classical description of the fibres of the isogeny $[n]$ on an
elliptic curve over an algebraically closed field of residue characteristic prime to $n$: a fibre is
empty or a translate of the $n$-torsion subgroup, which has $n^2$ elements. It underlies the
counting and valuation statements used in the construction of the Weil pairing, being cited by
[`WeierstrassCurve.Affine.ncard_fibSet`](thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.Affine.ncard_fibSet) and by the
lemmas on valuations of Weil functions.

